{"call_id":"toolu_01LqZn3bLheTwCu5bHzeJSWE","node_id":"implement","output":{"file":{"content":"# Antigravity CLI artifact-path configuration research\n\n## Finding\n\nAs of **Antigravity CLI 1.1.15, released August 19, 2026**, I found **no supported flag, `settings.json` key, environment variable, stream-JSON field, sandbox option, plugin manifest field, or artifact-review policy that changes the artifact root from the per-conversation `brain/\u003cuuid\u003e` directory to a workspace or `--add-dir` path**. The current implementation/documentation treats workspace paths and artifact paths as distinct namespaces. This is particularly explicit in the Hooks API, which passes hooks both `workspacePaths` and a separate `artifactDirectoryPath`. citeturn19view0turn20view0\n\nThere is, however, now a **documented solution for requirement (b)**: custom-agent frontmatter has a `tools:` allowlist. A custom agent selected with `--agent` can expose `run_command` while omitting `write_to_file`, `replace_file_content`, and `multi_replace_file_content`. Google explicitly documents `tools` as the list of tools permitted to the agent and documents custom agents as selectable as the primary CLI agent. citeturn16search5turn16search3\n\n| Mechanism | Result | Recommendation |\n|---|---|---|\n| Custom agent `tools:` allowlist | **Yes — removes native write tools from the agent toolset** | **Best solution** |\n| `PreToolUse` hook returning `\"decision\":\"deny\"` | **Yes — hard-blocks `write_to_file` calls** | Good fallback / dynamic policy |\n| `permissions.deny` for `write_file(...)` | Can block writes at permission layer | Weaker than removing the tool |\n| `--add-dir` | Workspace access only | Does **not** alter artifact root |\n| `artifactReviewPolicy: \"always-proceed\"` | Skips/automates artifact *review* | Does **not** relax artifact-path validation |\n| `--mode` | Changes approval/execution behavior | Does not redefine tools/artifact root |\n| `--sandbox` | Sandboxes terminal execution | Does not redefine artifacts |\n| Plugins | Add customizations/tools/hooks | No documented way to delete core tools globally |\n| `--input-format stream-json` | Multi-turn input transport only | No documented per-message tool/artifact controls |\n| Environment variable | None found for artifacts/tools | No solution for (a)/(c) |\n| Artifact-root override | **None found** | Upstream bug/feature request needed |\n| Suppress artifact declaration | **None found** | Upstream bug/feature request needed |\n\nThe key practical result is therefore: **run headless with a custom main agent whose `tools:` allowlist excludes the Cortex file writers.** This is substantially stronger than telling the model not to call them.\n\n## Supported mechanism: remove `write_to_file` with a custom agent\n\nCurrent Antigravity custom-agent documentation defines a `tools: string[]` frontmatter property as an **explicit list of permitted tools**. The same format has `mainAgent: true`, making the agent selectable as the primary agent rather than merely invocable as a subagent. Google’s custom-agent announcement explicitly says a `mainAgent` can be launched through `agy --agent \u003cname\u003e` and that its execution parameters, including the scoped toolset, are adopted by that main-agent session. citeturn16search5turn16search3\n\nFor your use case, create for example:\n\n`~/.gemini/config/agents/shell-writer/agent.md`\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: shell-writer\ndescription: Headless workspace agent that performs mutations through shell commands.\nmainAgent: true\nsubagent: false\ntools:\n  - view_file\n  - list_dir\n  - find_by_name\n  - grep_search\n  - run_command\n---\n\n# System Prompt\n\nPerform all workspace file creation and modification through run_command.\nDo not delegate file mutation to another agent.\n```\n\nGlobal agents are discovered under `~/.gemini/config/agents/\u003cname\u003e.md` or `~/.gemini/config/agents/\u003cname\u003e/agent.md`; workspace-local equivalents live under `.agents/agents/`. The docs specifically list `view_file`, `replace_file_content`, `grep_search`, and `run_command` as valid native tool identifiers, and the Hooks tool reference confirms the exact native identifier you need to omit is `write_to_file`. citeturn16search5turn20view0\n\nThen run:\n\n```bash\nagy \\\n  -p \"$PROMPT\" \\\n  --agent shell-writer \\\n  --output-format stream-json \\\n  --dangerously-skip-permissions \\\n  --add-dir \"$WORKSPACE\"\n```\n\nThe important part is not the system-prompt sentence; it is the **absence of `write_to_file` from `tools:`**. Also omit `replace_file_content` and `multi_replace_file_content` if you want *all* native file mutation forced through `run_command`. The official tool catalog identifies those three as native file-mutating operations; notably both `write_to_file` and `multi_replace_file_content` can carry `ArtifactMetadata`. citeturn20view0\n\nYou can make this mechanically testable in your driver. The `stream-json` `init` event exposes `init.tools`, documented as the names of **all available tools** for that run. Assert that `write_to_file` is absent before accepting the session. citeturn22search1turn23view4\n\nFor example:\n\n```bash\nagy \\\n  -p \"$PROMPT\" \\\n  --agent shell-writer \\\n  --output-format stream-json \\\n  --dangerously-skip-permissions \\\n  --add-dir \"$WORKSPACE\" |\njq -c '\n  if .event == \"init\" and\n     (.init.tools | index(\"write_to_file\")) != null\n  then\n    error(\"write_to_file unexpectedly enabled\")\n  else\n    .\n  end\n'\n```\n\nThis mechanism is much closer to a true “disable native write tool” switch than permission denial: **the tool is not advertised as part of the selected agent's allowed toolset**, so the model does not have to voluntarily obey a prompt telling it not to use the tool. Google introduced `--agent` in CLI 1.1.1 and Markdown-format custom agents in 1.1.6; the current documented `tools:` capability is confirmed in the current 1.1.15-era documentation. For production use of this workaround I would therefore pin **1.1.15 or later**, rather than trying to infer which intermediate build first acquired the current `tools:` semantics. citeturn19view4turn19view2turn16search5\n\nOne nuance: the current Subagents reference calls `tools` the explicit tool list “permitted for this subagent,” but the same document gives `mainAgent`, while Google's custom-agent announcement explicitly says a custom agent launched as the main CLI agent adopts its frontmatter execution parameters and demonstrates `tools:` as the scoped toolset. That combination is strong documentation that the restriction applies when selected through `--agent`, not only during `invoke_subagent`. citeturn16search5turn16search3\n\n## Secondary mechanism: hard-block it with a hook\n\nThere is a second documented enforcement surface that is useful if you cannot switch agents: a **`PreToolUse` hook** can match `write_to_file` by exact tool name and return:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"decision\": \"deny\",\n  \"reason\": \"Native write_to_file is disabled; use run_command for workspace writes.\"\n}\n```\n\nGoogle documents `\"deny\"` as a **hard block** that executes before the tool, and `PreToolUse.matcher` supports exact tool names or regexes. `write_to_file` is explicitly listed as a supported matcher target. citeturn20view0turn20view1\n\nA global configuration can be:\n\n`~/.gemini/config/hooks.json`\n\n```json\n{\n  \"no-native-workspace-writes\": {\n    \"PreToolUse\": [\n      {\n        \"matcher\": \"^write_to_file$\",\n        \"hooks\": [\n          {\n            \"type\": \"command\",\n            \"command\": \"~/.local/bin/agy-block-native-write\",\n            \"timeout\": 5\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nwith:\n\n`~/.local/bin/agy-block-native-write`\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\ncat \u003e/dev/null\n\nprintf '%s\\n' \\\n  '{\"decision\":\"deny\",\"reason\":\"write_to_file is disabled. Use run_command for workspace file creation.\"}'\n```\n\nHooks are documented under the workspace `.agents/` customization directory or global `~/.gemini/config/`; the CLI changelog also records fixes specifically for workspace-local hooks and for disabled plugin hooks, confirming that this hook framework is part of the CLI customization path rather than only an IDE feature. citeturn20view3turn19view4turn19view2\n\nMore interestingly, you can make the rule **artifact-aware** instead of globally disabling the tool. Every hook receives the runtime's actual `artifactDirectoryPath` as well as the mounted `workspacePaths`, so you can allow `write_to_file` only when the target is underneath the brain artifact directory and reject it everywhere else. citeturn20view0\n\nFor example, with `jq` available:\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\nset -eu\n\ninput=\"$(cat)\"\ntarget=\"$(printf '%s' \"$input\" | jq -r '.toolCall.args.TargetFile // \"\"')\"\nartifact_root=\"$(printf '%s' \"$input\" | jq -r '.artifactDirectoryPath // \"\"')\"\n\ncase \"$target\" in\n  \"$artifact_root\"/*)\n    printf '%s\\n' '{\"decision\":\"allow\"}'\n    ;;\n  *)\n    printf '%s\\n' \\\n      '{\"decision\":\"deny\",\"reason\":\"write_to_file outside the conversation artifact directory is disabled; use run_command for workspace files.\"}'\n    ;;\nesac\n```\n\nThat is essentially the missing “scope `write_to_file` to artifact storage” policy implemented at the hook layer.\n\nI would still choose the **custom-agent allowlist** first. A hook denies the model *after* it proposes the call, so the agent has to recover from the denial and choose `run_command`; a tool allowlist prevents it from seeing `write_to_file` in the first place. The stream `init.tools` field gives you a particularly clean way to verify the latter invariant. citeturn20view1turn23view4\n\nThere is one caveat around `--dangerously-skip-permissions`: official headless documentation says that flag changes the effective permission mode to `always-proceed` and auto-approves tool permission requests, while Hooks documentation describes `PreToolUse` as a separate pre-execution gate whose `\"deny\"` result hard-blocks execution. I found **no documentation saying `--dangerously-skip-permissions` bypasses hooks**; the documented architecture therefore indicates the hook should still gate the tool, but unlike the `tools:` allowlist I did not find an explicit sentence testing that exact combination. citeturn23view4turn20view1\n\nA third, less attractive option is permission policy. Antigravity's permission language has separate `write_file(...)` and `command(...)` resource actions, and current headless mode respects persisted permissions. Thus, without `--dangerously-skip-permissions`, native writes can be denied while selected shell commands are allowed. However, this acts at the permission layer rather than removing the tool and is therefore inferior for your failure mode. Headless-mode settings were specifically fixed in **1.1.4** so persisted permissions, file-access, sandbox, auto-execution and artifact-review settings are honored. citeturn4view1turn19view3turn23view4\n\n## Why the other configuration surfaces do not fix the artifact root\n\nThe core mismatch is visible in Google's own interfaces. `/add-dir` is documented only as adding a path to the **active workspace**. Separately, the Hooks API exposes `workspacePaths` and `artifactDirectoryPath` as distinct fields; the latter is described as the directory containing the conversation's artifacts and screenshots. This strongly indicates that `--add-dir` changes file-access/workspace scope, not the artifact namespace. citeturn22search6turn20view0\n\nThe permission docs reinforce that distinction: file reads and writes inside active workspaces are automatically allowed, and permission rules speak in terms such as `write_file(src/)`. Thus a path can be perfectly valid for a filesystem tool from the **permissions** perspective while still being invalid as an **artifact**. That is exactly consistent with your observation: the physical write succeeds, then conversion/declaration of the tool call as an artifact rejects the same pathname. citeturn23view4turn4view1\n\n### `settings.json`\n\nThe published `settings.json` reference includes `toolPermission`, `artifactReviewPolicy`, `allowNonWorkspaceAccess`, `enableTerminalSandbox`, and the ordinary permissions structures, but exposes no `artifactRoot`, `artifactDirectory`, `brainDirectory`, artifact-path allowlist, or “do not declare artifacts” switch. `artifactReviewPolicy` has values controlling whether artifact review asks, lets the agent decide, or automatically proceeds; that is a **review policy**, not an alternate pathname validator. citeturn2view0\n\nThis distinction became especially clear in CLI 1.1.4, whose changelog says headless mode was fixed to honor persisted **artifact review** settings. So `\"artifactReviewPolicy\":\"always-proceed\"` is a real headless setting on current versions, but it only removes/reduces the review decision. Your error occurs earlier while converting/declaring the tool call because the pathname violates the artifact contract; there is no documented setting making `always-proceed` mean “accept malformed/out-of-root artifact metadata.” citeturn19view3\n\nThat also explains why the July report in issue #548 is not a solution. Its reporter tried both:\n\n```json\n\"toolPermission\": \"always-proceed\",\n\"artifactReviewPolicy\": \"always-proceed\"\n```\n\nand observed broken print-mode behavior. The later 1.1.4 fix addressed print mode not honoring persisted policies, but it did not announce any relaxation of artifact-path validity. citeturn24view0turn19view3\n\n### `--mode` and `--sandbox`\n\n`--mode=accept-edits` changes whether standard file operations such as `write_to_file`, `replace_file_content`, and `multi_replace_file_content` proceed automatically; it does not remove those tools or redefine where artifacts may live. In fact, Google's mode documentation explicitly lists those native writers as operations that become automatic in accept-edits mode. citeturn4view0\n\n`--sandbox` and `enableTerminalSandbox` concern terminal/process isolation. They are not a second filesystem/artifact-root mechanism, and Google's documentation describes them in terms of sandboxed shell execution rather than artifact storage. citeturn4view3\n\n### Plugins and agents\n\nPlugin manifests are additive customization packages containing skills, agents, rules, MCP configuration and hooks. The documented plugin manifest itself only identifies the package; there is no core-tool-removal field in the plugin manifest. A plugin can therefore supply the **custom agent** or **hook** described above, but I found no plugin setting that globally redefines `write_to_file` or the artifact root. citeturn4view2\n\nThere is an interesting internal precedent: the model-facing `define_subagent` tool has an `enable_write_tools` argument. An official CLI issue shows a dynamically defined subagent being created with:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"enable_write_tools\": false\n}\n```\n\nand the current official Hooks tool catalog also documents `define_subagent(..., enable_write_tools, ...)`. That is an internal/dynamic-subagent capability, however; I found no documented static-agent frontmatter key named `enable_write_tools`. The supported declarative equivalent is now `tools:`. citeturn16search0turn16search1turn16search5\n\n### `--input-format stream-json`\n\n`--input-format stream-json` is brand new in **1.1.15**. The release notes describe it as reading newline-delimited JSON prompts from stdin and running one turn per message in a persistent conversation. I found no documented per-message artifact-root, permission-mode, or tool-selection field. In fact, the current Headless Mode page still documents the output stream extensively but has not yet incorporated an input-format schema or `--input-format` into its flag table, which appears to lag the release by one revision. citeturn19view0turn23view1turn23view4\n\nConsequently, there is no evidence that input NDJSON provides an undocumented analogue of an API `tools` field. For tool selection, the documented surface is the launch-time `--agent` plus that agent's `tools:` frontmatter. citeturn16search5turn23view4\n\n### Environment variables\n\nThe changelog documents environment-level controls such as `GEMINI_API_KEY`, `GOOGLE_GEMINI_BASE_URL`, `AGY_CLI_DISABLE_LATEX`, and `AGY_CLI_HIDE_ACCOUNT_INFO`, but I found no documented `AGY_*`, `GEMINI_*`, or Antigravity setting controlling `brain`, artifact root, artifact declaration, or native file-tool availability. citeturn19view1turn21search1\n\nThis negative source audit is necessarily somewhat bounded because the public `google-antigravity/antigravity-cli` repository does **not contain the CLI implementation source** in a form that lets us grep the validator or hidden environment-variable handling; the public repo mainly carries release/documentation material and binaries. That prevents a truly exhaustive source-code proof that no unsupported environment variable exists. The public API/configuration surfaces, current docs, release history, and issue tracker reveal none. citeturn10view0turn25search2\n\n## What the artifact validator appears to be doing\n\nThe strongest clue is in the documented native tool schema itself. Google's Hooks reference describes `write_to_file` as:\n\n```text\nwrite_to_file\n  TargetFile\n  Overwrite\n  CodeContent\n  Description\n  IsArtifact             optional\n  ArtifactMetadata       optional\n```\n\nand `multi_replace_file_content` likewise accepts optional `ArtifactMetadata`. citeturn20view0\n\nAt the same time, the runtime exposes a dedicated `artifactDirectoryPath`, distinct from `workspacePaths`. citeturn20view0\n\nThat makes your failure highly consistent with this sequence:\n\n1. `TargetFile=/workspace/foo.md` is accepted by the ordinary file-write/permission layer because `/workspace` is active through `--add-dir`.\n2. The Cortex call also carries artifact semantics — likely `IsArtifact` and/or `ArtifactMetadata`, or is subsequently interpreted as an artifact-bearing call.\n3. The permission/declaration converter validates that metadata against the conversation's `artifactDirectoryPath`.\n4. The converter discovers that `/workspace/foo.md` is not inside `~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/\u003cconversation-id\u003e/` and returns `invalid_args`.\n5. Because this happens while converting/declarating the model tool call, the otherwise successful filesystem mutation is followed by a failed turn.\n\nSteps 2–4 are an inference from your exact error plus the official tool/hook schemas, rather than publicly inspectable implementation code. The inference is reinforced by 1.1.15's release note: Google fixed artifacts in **subdirectories of “a conversation's artifact directory”** not appearing in the artifact list. That wording strongly confirms that artifact identity remains rooted in one conversation-specific directory; the change permits `brain/\u003cuuid\u003e/scratch/...`, not arbitrary workspace roots. citeturn19view0turn20view0\n\nThat 1.1.15 change is important for your case because it means the release shipped **today with artifact-directory work**, but it did not announce workspace artifact roots. I would therefore not expect upgrading from 1.1.14 to 1.1.15 to make `\u003cworkspace\u003e/foo.md` a legal artifact. citeturn19view0\n\nThe ideal product behavior is probably not to make every `--add-dir` an artifact directory. A cleaner fix would be for ordinary workspace `write_to_file` calls to remain ordinary workspace writes and for the converter to apply `ArtifactMetadata` validation **only when the call is actually declaring an artifact**. The fact that `IsArtifact` is an optional field in the official tool schema makes that separation particularly natural. citeturn20view0\n\n## Version and deployment recommendation\n\nFor a headless orchestrator, I would pin **1.1.15+** and use the custom-agent tool allowlist.\n\nThe relevant release history is:\n\n| Capability | Version |\n|---|---:|\n| `--agent` selection | 1.1.1 |\n| Headless settings/permissions/artifact-review policies correctly honored | 1.1.4 |\n| Markdown `agent.md` custom agents | 1.1.6 |\n| `--output-format stream-json` with machine-readable tool events | 1.1.8 |\n| `inheritCustomizations` for Markdown agents | 1.1.14 |\n| `--input-format stream-json` | **1.1.15** |\n| Artifact discovery in subdirectories of the conversation artifact directory fixed | **1.1.15** |\n\nThese version points come directly from Google's CLI changelog. citeturn19view4turn19view3turn19view2turn19view0\n\nA hardened invocation would therefore look like:\n\n```bash\nagy \\\n  -p \"$PROMPT\" \\\n  --agent shell-writer \\\n  --output-format stream-json \\\n  --dangerously-skip-permissions \\\n  --add-dir \"$WORKSPACE\"\n```\n\nwith an initialization assertion:\n\n```text\ninit.tools MUST NOT contain:\n  write_to_file\n  replace_file_content\n  multi_replace_file_content\n```\n\nand ideally:\n\n```text\ninit.tools MUST contain:\n  run_command\n```\n\n`init.tools` is explicitly the effective list of available tools, so this turns a configuration assumption into an invariant your orchestrator can enforce before trusting the turn. citeturn22search1turn23view4\n\nFor a belt-and-suspenders deployment, add the `PreToolUse` hook as well. That protects you against an agent/tool-filtering regression: if a future version unexpectedly advertises `write_to_file`, the hook can still hard-deny its execution. citeturn20view1\n\n## Upstream bug to file\n\nI found **no currently indexed official CLI issue containing the exact diagnostic** `\"is not a valid artifact path\"` / `\"artifacts must be in\"` for this workspace-write case. The closest official report is issue **#548**, concerning headless permission policies and artifact review settings, but its main print-mode-policy defect was subsequently addressed by the 1.1.4 change that made headless runs honor persisted settings. Your problem is distinct and merits a new bug rather than being folded into #548. citeturn25search0turn19view3\n\nI would file it against `google-antigravity/antigravity-cli` as:\n\n\u003e **Bug: `write_to_file` succeeds in `--add-dir` workspace, then artifact declaration rejects the same path as outside `brain/\u003cuuid\u003e`**\n\nA minimal report should include this reproducer:\n\n```bash\nworkspace=\"$(mktemp -d)\"\n\nagy \\\n  -p \"Create $workspace/foo.md using write_to_file with the contents hello.\" \\\n  --add-dir \"$workspace\" \\\n  --dangerously-skip-permissions \\\n  --output-format stream-json\n```\n\nExpected:\n\n```text\nEither:\n\n1. write_to_file(TargetFile=\u003cworkspace\u003e/foo.md) is treated as an ordinary\n   workspace file write and the turn succeeds without artifact metadata;\n\nor\n\n2. the model/tool schema prevents ArtifactMetadata/IsArtifact from being\n   attached to paths outside the conversation artifact directory;\n\nor\n\n3. the CLI exposes a documented way to disable artifact declaration or\n   configure the artifact root.\n```\n\nActual:\n\n```text\nThe file is successfully written to \u003cworkspace\u003e/foo.md, but the turn\nsubsequently fails while declaring/converting permissions:\n\ndeclaring permissions: cortex tool write_to_file:\nconvert tool call for permissions:\nmodel output error:\ninvalid tool call error (invalid_args)\n\u003cworkspace\u003e/foo.md is not a valid artifact path;\nartifacts must be in\n~/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/\u003cconversation-id\u003e/\n```\n\nThe particularly useful implementation-level question for Google is:\n\n\u003e **Why is `ArtifactMetadata` validation being applied to a successful non-brain `write_to_file` call when the tool schema separately has optional `IsArtifact` / `ArtifactMetadata`, and workspace writes are independently legal?**\n\nThat question is grounded directly in Google's published tool schema and its separation of `workspacePaths` from `artifactDirectoryPath`. citeturn20view0\n\nI would also include the `stream-json` tool event for the successful `write_to_file`, followed by the terminal `result.status=ERROR`, because Google's structured-output contract exposes the canonical tool name, parameters and output specifically for diagnosing this kind of split success/failure. citeturn23view4\n\n**Bottom line:** there is no artifact-root override or “ignore artifact declaration errors” setting I could find. But current Antigravity now gives you a supported way around the bug that is stronger than prompt engineering: define a custom main agent with an explicit `tools:` allowlist, omit all native write tools, launch it using `--agent`, and verify from `stream-json` that `write_to_file` is absent. A `PreToolUse` hard-deny hook is the second-best enforcement mechanism. citeturn16search5turn16search3turn20view1turn23view4","filePath":"/Users/tyler/src/tractor/ephemeral/deep-research-report-2.md","numLines":372,"startLine":1,"totalLines":372},"type":"text"},"ts":"2026-08-19T22:13:46.71106Z","type":"tool_result"}
